6.
PMBRS uses a globally aligned fixed 60-second window grid as the canonical temporal reference system for all modalities. All sensor streams, sparse events, semantic artifacts, and derived representations are deterministically projected onto this shared timeline using modality-specific alignment rules. Higher-resolution, lower-resolution, event-driven, and multi-scale analyses are treated as derived views over the canonical timeline rather than independent temporal systems. This constraint exists to preserve cross-modality coherence, reproducibility, lineage consistency, and stable artifact interoperability across the platform.

7.
PMBRS adopts a raw-preserving artifact architecture in which behaviorally meaningful raw events and streams are retained whenever feasible to minimize irreversible information loss and support future reprocessing, retraining, and representation-learning experimentation. Canonical windows provide the aligned operational backbone for downstream modeling, while derived artifacts such as features, embeddings, clusters, and evaluations remain reproducible rather than authoritative. Exhaustive surveillance artifacts (e.g., screenshots, raw audio, full page contents) are excluded by default unless explicitly required for future research objectives.

8.
PMBRS modalities are structured time-indexed information sources that can be deterministically aligned to the canonical temporal system through modality-specific transformation rules. Modalities are not treated as semantically equivalent and are instead categorized by epistemological role, including observational, declarative, subjective, and inferred modalities. All modalities must conform to shared structural alignment contracts while preserving lineage, uncertainty, and modality-specific semantics. Core behavioral representations prioritize observational modalities, while subjective and inferred modalities remain partially separated to prevent semantic leakage and preserve interpretability.

9.
PMBRS uses a multi-layer authority model that distinguishes between observational evidence, canonical operational structure, and derived analytical interpretation. Authoritative artifacts consist only of directly observed, user-declared, or user-authored records, including raw behavioral events, wearable and telemetry streams, journal text, intervention declarations, calendar/context artifacts, timestamps, and canonical aligned windows. These artifacts represent irreducible historical evidence and form the permanent source-of-truth layer for the system.
Canonical windows serve as the operational temporal backbone of PMBRS by providing the globally aligned reference structure through which all modalities are synchronized and compared. While derived from upstream observational artifacts, canonical windows are treated as stable operational infrastructure rather than interpretive outputs.
All downstream analytical products—including features, embeddings, behavioral regimes, inferred events, evaluations, semantic summaries, sentiment analysis, and LLM-generated interpretations—are treated as non-authoritative derived artifacts. These outputs are model-dependent, reproducible, versioned, replaceable, and fully lineage-traceable to upstream authoritative artifacts. Derived outputs must never overwrite or recursively redefine historical source data.
Inferred behavioral events and semantic interpretations are treated as probabilistic hypotheses rather than ground truth. They may support experimentation, comparison, and interpretation, but must preserve uncertainty, confidence estimates, and transformation lineage.
This authority structure exists to preserve reproducibility, prevent recursive semantic contamination, support future retraining and reinterpretation, maintain clear epistemological boundaries between observation and interpretation, and ensure that PMBRS remains grounded in observable behavioral evidence rather than opaque model assertions.
10.
All canonical temporal windows exist structurally regardless of modality completeness, but windows carry explicit metadata describing modality coverage, signal quality, uncertainty, and missingness semantics. Window validity is therefore treated as a confidence-bearing analytical property rather than a binary existence condition. Downstream systems determine analytical suitability based on modality-specific completeness and quality thresholds appropriate to the task being performed. Dense observational modalities primarily determine behavioral reliability, while sparse semantic modalities act as contextual overlays rather than hard validity requirements.

