PMBRS adopts an artifact-oriented, immutable-first, lineage-centric architecture in which all subsystems communicate exclusively through formally defined artifacts rather than implicit internal state or direct subsystem coupling. Artifacts constitute the fundamental operational objects of the platform and serve as the backbone for reproducibility, experimentation, interoperability, and long-term behavioral analysis.
Artifact Definition
An artifact is any structured object produced, consumed, stored, transformed, or referenced within PMBRS. Artifacts are first-class system entities rather than unstructured data blobs and are governed through explicit schema contracts, lineage relationships, and metadata requirements.
Each artifact contains:
Artifact type identifier
Globally unique identity
Creation metadata
Lineage references
Schema version information
Configuration and execution references
Modality references where applicable
Provenance metadata describing inputs, creators, parameters, and execution environment
Artifact identity defines semantic equivalence independently from physical storage location.

