Experimentation System Formalization
PMBRS adopts a structured temporal intervention framework in which experiments are treated as first-class, lineage-traceable behavioral artifacts rather than informal annotations or automated causal claims. The experimentation subsystem exists to support longitudinal behavioral refinement, structured self-experimentation, and evidence-informed comparison of interventions, contexts, and behavioral patterns using multimodal temporal data.
The experimentation framework is designed to preserve scientific humility, reproducibility, interpretability, and low operational friction while avoiding deterministic behavioral prescription, uncontrolled narrative generation, or unsupported causal inference.

Experiment Definition
An experiment is a temporally scoped declaration that one or more intentionally introduced variables may influence downstream behavioral structure, subjective experience, or evaluative metrics.
Experiments are declarative rather than inferred. Authoritative experiments originate from explicit user declarations rather than automated system assumptions. The system may later generate inferred behavioral shifts or contextual hypotheses, but inferred patterns are treated as probabilistic analytical artifacts rather than authoritative experiments.
Experiments function as temporally aligned contextual structures that condition interpretation, evaluation, and longitudinal comparison throughout PMBRS.

