Evaluation Operationalization
PMBRS treats evaluation as a first-class, lineage-traceable subsystem responsible for validating system integrity, representation quality, experimental usefulness, longitudinal stability, and interpretive reliability across the platform. Evaluation is not treated as auxiliary model monitoring or isolated performance metrics, but as a reproducible analytical framework embedded directly into the architecture of the system.
The purpose of evaluation within PMBRS is to ensure that behavioral representations, derived analyses, and experimental conclusions remain statistically meaningful, structurally stable, reproducible, uncertainty-aware, and behaviorally useful without overclaiming semantic or causal truth.

Evaluation Philosophy
PMBRS evaluates whether the system produces stable, reproducible, behaviorally informative structure that supports useful longitudinal experimentation and interpretation rather than whether it infers psychological ground truth.
Because PMBRS operates without authoritative behavioral labels or deterministic cognitive-state targets, evaluation primarily relies on:
structural consistency,
temporal coherence,
comparative usefulness,
robustness,
reproducibility,
intervention sensitivity,
and longitudinal stability.
Evaluation therefore emphasizes proxy validation and behavioral utility rather than classical supervised accuracy metrics.

