PMBRS adopts a local-first privacy architecture in which authoritative behavioral artifacts, derived analytical artifacts, and longitudinal historical records are stored locally by default under direct user control. The system is designed primarily as a personal behavioral experimentation and representation-learning platform rather than a networked or cloud-native service architecture.
The default operational assumption of PMBRS is that all sensitive behavioral data remains on-device unless explicitly exported, synchronized, or shared by the user. Cloud synchronization, remote storage, distributed processing, and third-party persistence are considered optional future extensions rather than foundational architectural assumptions.
PMBRS intentionally minimizes raw surveillance collection and prioritizes structured behavioral representations, aggregated temporal features, semantic abstractions, and reproducible derived artifacts over exhaustive raw capture. High-risk surveillance modalities—including continuous screenshots, full page contents, raw audio archives, keystroke logging, and unrestricted message capture—are excluded by default unless explicitly enabled for narrowly scoped future research purposes.
Sensitive modalities within PMBRS include, but are not limited to:
journal text,
search behavior,
browser telemetry,
LLM interaction artifacts,
wearable physiological streams,
intervention history,
calendar/context metadata,
and derived semantic interpretations.
These modalities are treated as highly sensitive personal behavioral records and must preserve strict lineage separation between authoritative evidence and probabilistic interpretation.
PMBRS distinguishes between authoritative artifacts and external provider visibility. External systems—including wearable vendors, browser platforms, OCR systems, LLM providers, media services, and synchronization infrastructure—are treated as untrusted or partially trusted integration boundaries. PMBRS therefore attempts to minimize unnecessary provider exposure by preferring local preprocessing, local storage, derived representations, and selective semantic extraction whenever feasible.
Frontend interfaces, dashboards, visualizations, notifications, and mobile integrations are treated as downstream applications with restricted artifact visibility rather than unrestricted access layers. Operational interfaces should expose only the minimum required information necessary for interpretation, experimentation, anomaly review, or user interaction.
PMBRS is not designed as a multi-user collaborative platform and therefore does not currently prioritize enterprise-grade authentication, role-based access control, organizational tenancy, or internet-facing service architecture. The system instead assumes a personal single-user trust model centered around local ownership and direct physical device control.
Deletion semantics preserve the distinction between authoritative historical evidence and reproducible derived artifacts. Derived artifacts, caches, indexes, embeddings, summaries, evaluations, and transient analytical outputs may be safely regenerated or discarded without affecting authoritative historical evidence. Authoritative artifacts may be deleted only through explicit user-directed operations, with lineage-aware invalidation of dependent downstream artifacts where applicable.
Security philosophy within PMBRS prioritizes:
local ownership,
minimal provider exposure,
deterministic reproducibility,
