PMBRS adopts a lineage-preserving, schema-versioned artifact architecture designed to support long-term reproducibility, safe system evolution, historical integrity, and future reinterpretation of behavioral data and analytical outputs.
All artifacts within PMBRS are required to embed explicit schema version metadata identifying the structural assumptions, field definitions, transformation semantics, and compatibility expectations under which the artifact was produced. Schema versioning is treated as a mandatory constitutional requirement rather than an implementation convenience.
PMBRS distinguishes between authoritative historical artifacts and reproducible derived artifacts during schema evolution. Authoritative artifacts—including raw observational events, journal entries, intervention declarations, timestamps, canonical temporal windows, and user-authored records—are treated as immutable historical evidence and are never rewritten retroactively during schema migrations except under explicit user-directed corrective operations.
Derived analytical artifacts—including features, embeddings, clustering outputs, semantic summaries, evaluations, inferred events, and interpretive outputs—are treated as reproducible products of upstream artifacts, transformation logic, configuration state, and model versions. These artifacts may be regenerated under updated schemas, models, or analytical pipelines while preserving lineage continuity and historical traceability.
PMBRS therefore adopts a regeneration-over-rewrite evolution philosophy. When schemas evolve:
authoritative artifacts persist unchanged,
lineage continuity is preserved,
derived artifacts may be recomputed under updated schemas,
historical artifacts remain interpretable under their original schema definitions,
and old analytical outputs remain queryable and reproducible where feasible.
Backward compatibility requirements are concentrated primarily within the constitutional layers of the system, including:
canonical temporal semantics,
artifact identity rules,
lineage semantics,
modality contracts,
authoritative artifact definitions,
schema version metadata,
and canonical window alignment behavior.
Higher-level analytical systems—including embeddings, clustering systems, feature pipelines, evaluation methods, interpretive layers, visualization systems, and semantic abstractions—are intentionally treated as replaceable and evolution-friendly subsystems.
Deprecated analytical systems, feature pipelines, embedding architectures, clustering methods, and interpretive layers are not treated as invalid historical states. Instead, deprecated systems remain lineage-traceable, historically reproducible where feasible, and queryable for longitudinal comparison and experiment continuity, even after they stop producing new outputs.
Schema evolution within PMBRS prioritizes:
preservation of historical integrity,
reproducibility of prior analyses,
deterministic lineage continuity,
controlled migration semantics,
safe subsystem replacement,
and long-term behavioral archive stability.
This governance philosophy exists to ensure that PMBRS remains sustainable as a multi-year behavioral experimentation and representation-learning platform without sacrificing interpretability, reproducibility, or historical coherence as schemas, models, analytical methods, and subsystem implementations evolve over time.